2 CONC ils on lineage history, which also helps explain the ancestral burial cite at Xuzhou (Yingchuan), Changshe county. Zhen's [634], Jiang's [636], Yi(1)'s [649], Zong(1)'s [657], and Wei's [8051] father. Zongzhi's [659], Zonggu's [66
2 CONC 0], Zongliang's [661], Zongshi's [662], Zongdao's [663], and Zongyan's [664] grandfather. Huang's [648], Lian's [7230] and Qiu's [632] great grandfather, Zhao's [633] great great grandfather, and Yuanji's [669] and Yuanlong's [670
2 CONC ] direct ancestor. Yi(2) was Wang(2) Dan's [8153] son-in-law and Su(1) Shunbin's [7065] father-in-law. His father, Baoshu, used knowledge of the classics (mingjing) to travel from the ancestral home in Zhending, Lingshou county to
2 CONC  Kaifeng and established the family here. Yi died in his private home in Kaifeng, but was buried in Xuzhou, Changshe xian (some descendants identified as Changshe residents, but this was lineage ancestral burial grounds). His son 
2 CONC Wei(2)'s [3329] father-in-law was Li(2) Ruogu [1046]. Wei(2)'s son, Zonghou [3328], used Li(2) Ruogu's yin to enter the civil service. Zhang Fangping, WJ, 37.9a-17a, 39.1a-8b; Jinshi cui, 142.2b; Liu Ban, WJ, 39.2a; Su Shunqin, WJ
2 CONC , 15.222-224 (wife, born Wang, who died in 1027 at age 44), 16.237-244. CBD, 5, 4152-4154. — [https://www.geni.com/projects/CBDB-Hartwell-collection/46406 RMH]
