1 NOTE {geni:about_me} [https://cbdb.fas.harvard.edu/cbdbapi/person.php?id=13093 '''Lü Mengqi''' 呂蒙奇] Lu(9) Mengqi [13093] Based on XTS, 75A.52b and Lu(9) Mengzheng's [8101] and Lu(9) Yijian's [8100] funerary inscriptions, the evidence s
2 CONC uggests the following pattern of migration. The Dongping Lu(9) ancestor setttled in Shouzhou, which was a part of Dongping jun. Mengqi was Bingbu shilang, Beijing fu liushou (resident vice-prefect of the Northern Capital) at the e
2 CONC nd of the Tang and settled in Taiyuan (Taiyuan was the Tang dynasty Beijing). One son, Guitu [13094], moved to Luoyang at the beginning of Song, whereas his brother, Guixiang [13096], held a post in Shouzhou, Shouchun (which happe
2 CONC ned to be the ancestral home of the Dongping Lu(9)) and settled here. His three sons, Mengheng [7054], Mengsun [13097], and Mengzhou [13098] apparently moved to Kaifeng (Mengheng's son Yijian documented as moving to Kaifeng, but p
2 CONC robably his father migrated with son, since Mengsun's daughter designated as a Kaifeng resident). I record Mengheng as having moved Kaifeng from Shouchun and identify his two younger brothers, Mengsun and Mengzhou, as Kaifeng resi
2 CONC dents and all their descendants (before other moves) are designated as Kaifeng residents, whereas all descendants of Mengzheng are designated as Luoyang residents. — [https://www.geni.com/projects/CBDB-Hartwell-collection/46406 
2 CONC RMH]
